---
title: Setting up specimen records
sidebar:
  label: Setup
  order: 1
---

NAHPU's default controlled vocabularies for `Specimen Types` and `Treatments`
are intentionally short. Before entering a large batch of specimens, configure
your numbering rules and controlled vocabularies so your team records
consistent data in the field or collection room.

{% steps %}

1. Open your project, open the `Dashboard` menu in the top-left corner, and
   select `Settings`. (Opening the project first is required to edit a
   project's `Project ID` values.)
2. Under `Catalogs`, select `Specimens`.
3. Under `Field ID`, choose how new specimens are numbered:

    - `Personnel ID` (default): the cataloger's initials and their own running
      number. Add a person with the `Cataloger` role to the project and assign
      them a current field number.
    - `Project ID`: a `Prefix`, `Current catalog number`, and `Suffix`, joined
      directly. Turn on `Auto-increment catalog number` to advance the number
      each time a specimen uses it.

    Select `Save field ID settings` to apply `Project ID` changes.

    This sets the default only. Open `Edit field ID` on any specimen to switch
    between `Personnel` and `Project` for that record. A project can contain
    both kinds, but one specimen cannot carry both identifiers.

    {% aside type="caution" %}

    Changing a `Project ID` prefix or suffix changes how existing project IDs
    display, and NAHPU asks you to confirm. Check first whether those IDs have
    already been exported or written on specimen labels.

    {% /aside %}

4. Configure `Tissue ID` (optional).

    Some collections assign separate tissue numbers for the same voucher
    specimen. Enter a prefix, the next number, and a suffix here. Leading zeros
    are preserved, so `M0042-T` is followed by `M0043-T`. NAHPU can then
    generate the next tissue number when you add a specimen part.

    When the active catalog format is `Ichthyology`, `Individual tag ID` sets
    up the same kind of numbering for individuals in a fish lot.

5. Populate `Specimen Types`.

    Add every voucher preparation and tissue type you expect to use, including
    types you collect only occasionally.

6. Populate `Treatments`.

    Treatments apply to specimen parts. Defaults include `None`, `ETOH`,
    `Formalin`, `LN2`, and `DMSO`.

{% /steps %}

The same screen also includes `Capture records` (`Always show collector field`),
the restricted `Sex` controlled vocabulary, the controlled vocabularies for
`Identification Methods`, `Life Stages`, and `Conditions`, and `Symbionts`
settings (for `Mammalogy`, `Ornithology`, `Herpetology`, and `Ichthyology`).
Review them before the field season as well.

{% aside type="tip" %}

Controlled vocabularies are user configs shared by every project on this
device. Use `Export user configs` and `Import user configs` to keep every
NAHPU installation on the same controlled vocabularies.

See [Settings](/en/usages/settings/) for the full configuration overview.

{% /aside %}
